119
00:08:35,109 --> 00:08:37,960
Good morning and welcome
back to Ashcombe Today.

120
00:08:38,709 --> 00:08:42,960
Now, those small metal disks that have
appeared in the town centre pavements

121
00:08:43,010 --> 00:08:47,960
are part of the new Ashcombe
Heritage Trail, which opens this Saturday.

122
00:08:48,509 --> 00:08:52,960
And here to tell us all about it is the
council's heritage officer, Ruth Penhale.

123
00:08:52,961 --> 00:08:53,960
Morning, Ruth.

124
00:08:53,961 --> 00:08:56,960
Morning, Steve. And thanks for having me.

125
00:08:56,961 --> 00:09:01,960
Well, the trail links 14 places
of interest in the old town.

126
00:09:02,060 --> 00:09:05,960
But this morning, I'll just
pick out six of the main ones.

127
00:09:06,559 --> 00:09:08,960
Let's start at the Corn
Exchange on the High Street.

128
00:09:09,359 --> 00:09:12,960
A lot of people still think of
it as the place to get a coffee.

129
00:09:12,961 --> 00:09:15,960
And there was a cafe on
the ground floor for years.

130
00:09:15,961 --> 00:09:19,960
But it closed last spring and
the space is empty for now.

131
00:09:20,809 --> 00:09:23,810
What you will find just
inside the entrance hall

132
00:09:23,834 --> 00:09:26,972
is a model of the town
as it looked in about 1850.

133
00:09:27,109 --> 00:09:31,960
It was built by pupils from Ashcombe
Primary as a class project last term.

134
00:09:31,961 --> 00:09:33,960
And it's remarkably detailed.

135
00:09:33,961 --> 00:09:37,960
They even put tiny sheep
in the market square.

136
00:09:38,909 --> 00:09:42,960
From there, the trail goes
down Well Lane to St. Brideswell.

137
00:09:43,559 --> 00:09:45,960
This is one of the
oldest sights in the town.

138
00:09:46,359 --> 00:09:48,960
There's been a spring
there since medieval times.

139
00:09:49,609 --> 00:09:52,602
We'd originally planned to
put one of our audio posts

140
00:09:52,626 --> 00:09:55,972
there, where you press a
button and hear a short recording.

141
00:09:55,973 --> 00:09:58,960
But the budget didn't
stretch that far, sadly.

142
00:09:59,259 --> 00:10:02,632
And I should warn
listeners, the well sits at the

143
00:10:02,656 --> 00:10:05,972
bottom of a flight of
very narrow, uneven steps.

144
00:10:06,109 --> 00:10:09,489
We did look into putting
in a ramp, but because the

145
00:10:09,513 --> 00:10:12,972
stonework is protected,
we weren't given permission.

146
00:10:13,060 --> 00:10:15,441
So, I'm afraid it isn't
a stop that anyone

147
00:10:15,465 --> 00:10:17,972
in a wheelchair will
be able to get down to.

148
00:10:18,809 --> 00:10:21,960
Just past the well, you come to
the Tannery Bridge over the river.

149
00:10:21,961 --> 00:10:25,832
Now, generations of Ashcombe
people have called it the Black Bridge

150
00:10:25,856 --> 00:10:29,972
because that's the colour it's been
for as long as anyone can remember.

151
00:10:31,960 --> 00:10:35,861
But when our conservation team
scraped back the layers of paint last

152
00:10:35,885 --> 00:10:39,967
year, they discovered the ironwork
had first been painted a deep green.

153
00:10:39,968 --> 00:10:41,960
And that's the shade you'll see there now.

154
00:10:42,559 --> 00:10:44,960
It's caused a few complaints, actually.

155
00:10:45,359 --> 00:10:47,960
Then, it's back up the
hill to the Market Cross.

156
00:10:47,961 --> 00:10:51,960
You'll see people climbing the steps
around the base, hoping for a view.

157
00:10:51,961 --> 00:10:54,960
But, to be honest, you
can't see much from there.

158
00:10:55,534 --> 00:10:56,960
The buildings are too close.

159
00:10:57,659 --> 00:11:00,960
What makes the cross
special is the bell at the top.

160
00:11:01,309 --> 00:11:04,960
The original was stolen in
the 1970s and never recovered.

161
00:11:05,809 --> 00:11:09,199
The one hanging there
now was cast last year by a

162
00:11:09,223 --> 00:11:12,972
foundry in Devon, copied
exactly from old photographs.

163
00:11:13,609 --> 00:11:17,181
Round the corner from the
cross are the Weavers Cottages, a

164
00:11:17,205 --> 00:11:20,972
row of five little houses where
cloth used to be made by hand.

165
00:11:21,409 --> 00:11:24,172
People are often disappointed
to learn that they're

166
00:11:24,196 --> 00:11:26,972
all private homes now,
so you can't just wander in.

167
00:11:31,159 --> 00:11:35,024
But the owners of the End Cottage have very
kindly agreed to let us in once a month,

168
00:11:35,048 --> 00:11:38,972
which means the only way to see inside is
to book a place on one of our guided walks.

169
00:11:39,459 --> 00:11:44,960
And finally, there's the old railway
goods shed down by the station car park.

170
00:11:47,259 --> 00:11:50,957
There's one of our audio posts inside,
and if you press the button, you'll hear

171
00:11:50,981 --> 00:11:54,972
recordings we made with men and women who
worked on the railway in the 1950s and 60s.

172
00:11:55,659 --> 00:11:58,960
A lot of people ask me whether
there's somewhere to eat in the shed.

173
00:11:59,359 --> 00:12:03,960
There isn't, I'm afraid, though the
Signal Box Cafe is just over the road.

174
00:12:04,109 --> 00:12:07,553
And if you're after a view
over the whole town, the best

175
00:12:07,577 --> 00:12:10,972
place is really the old water
tower behind the station.

176
00:12:11,509 --> 00:12:15,960
But that's not part of the trail,
because it isn't open to the public.

177
00:12:16,409 --> 00:12:19,520
Before you hear the
rest of the talk, you

178
00:12:19,544 --> 00:12:22,972
have some time to look
at questions 17 to 20.

179
00:12:53,209 --> 00:12:55,960
Ruth, tell us about those
discs in the pavement.

180
00:12:55,961 --> 00:12:58,960
Ah, the markers.

181
00:12:59,409 --> 00:13:01,960
Well, there was quite a debate about those.

182
00:13:02,010 --> 00:13:05,249
Some councillors wanted
them made from recycled

183
00:13:05,273 --> 00:13:07,972
plastic to be
environmentally friendly.

184
00:13:08,459 --> 00:13:12,960
But in the end, we went for bronze,
because it lasts so much longer.

185
00:13:13,409 --> 00:13:17,972
They were designed by Ellen Marsh, a sculptor
who's lived in Ashcombe all her life.

186
00:13:17,973 --> 00:13:21,960
They shouldn't need
replacing for a very long time.

187
00:13:22,010 --> 00:13:24,960
We're hoping they'll
still be there in 50 years.

188
00:13:25,309 --> 00:13:28,960
As for how long it takes,
the leaflet says 45 minutes.

189
00:13:28,961 --> 00:13:32,960
But that's if you walk
briskly and don't stop at all.

190
00:13:33,609 --> 00:13:37,960
If you actually read the information
boards, I'd allow about an hour and a half.

191
00:13:38,909 --> 00:13:41,960
Some people make a whole morning
of it and have lunch at the end.

192
00:13:41,961 --> 00:13:43,960
But that's up to you.

193
00:13:44,559 --> 00:13:46,960
There's also a free
app to go with the trail.

194
00:13:47,609 --> 00:13:49,960
Some people tell me they
prefer it to the printed leaflet.

195
00:13:49,961 --> 00:13:54,960
But personally, I find the map in
the leaflet much easier to follow.

196
00:13:55,609 --> 00:13:57,960
There's a paid version of
the app with extra content too.

197
00:13:58,209 --> 00:14:02,960
But honestly, the free one has
everything most visitors would need.

198
00:14:03,709 --> 00:14:09,960
My main piece of advice is this: the mobile
signal in the old streets is terrible.

199
00:14:10,509 --> 00:14:13,372
So, if you want to use
the app, make sure you've

200
00:14:13,396 --> 00:14:15,972
got it on your phone
before you leave home.

201
00:14:16,659 --> 00:14:18,960
And is there any way
listeners can get involved?

202
00:14:18,961 --> 00:14:23,960
Well, we've had a wonderful response
to our appeal for volunteer guides.

203
00:14:23,961 --> 00:14:27,519
In fact, we've got all the
guides we need for this

204
00:14:27,543 --> 00:14:30,972
year, so thank you to
everyone who came forward.

205
00:14:31,484 --> 00:14:35,972
Last year, we asked people to suggest
buildings to include, and that went well too.

206
00:14:36,709 --> 00:14:38,960
What we're short of now is pictures.

207
00:14:39,960 --> 00:14:43,890
If you've got old photographs of
the town in a drawer at home, shops,

208
00:14:43,914 --> 00:14:47,967
street parties, anything, we'd love
to scan them for the trail website.

209
00:14:47,968 --> 00:14:50,960
You can drop them at the library
and we'll return them within a week.

210
00:14:50,961 --> 00:14:51,961
Lovely.

211
00:14:52,259 --> 00:14:54,960
Ruth Penhale, thank you very much.

212
00:14:54,961 --> 00:14:57,960
That is the end of part two.

