220
00:16:53,145 --> 00:16:56,972
questions, so we really need to
decide what goes in and what comes out.

221
00:16:57,159 --> 00:17:00,960
Yeah, fair enough. I made a list
last night. Shall we go through it?

222
00:17:00,961 --> 00:17:01,961
Go on.

223
00:17:02,209 --> 00:17:05,242
Right. First, Chomsky
and the idea that we're

224
00:17:05,266 --> 00:17:07,972
born with some kind
of built-in grammar.

225
00:17:11,009 --> 00:17:14,872
Hmm. I mean, it's important, obviously,
but everyone in the group did that to

226
00:17:14,896 --> 00:17:18,972
death in first year. If we spend five
minutes on it, people will just switch off.

227
00:17:19,309 --> 00:17:24,960
True. So we cut it then. We can mention his
name in passing, but not give it a section.

228
00:17:25,459 --> 00:17:27,960
Next, kids who grow up with two languages.

229
00:17:29,034 --> 00:17:32,960
I was wondering about dropping
that, actually. It's such a huge area.

230
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:38,001
Ah, no. I really think we need it. Half
the people in our seminar group grew

231
00:17:38,025 --> 00:17:41,972
up speaking more than one language
at home. They'll want to hear about it.

232
00:17:41,973 --> 00:17:45,960
Fair point. Keep it, but keep it short.
What's next?

233
00:17:45,961 --> 00:17:50,960
Children learning sign language. There's
that brilliant study from Nicaragua.

234
00:17:51,960 --> 00:17:55,979
I'd love to include that, but
realistically, we'd have to explain

235
00:17:56,003 --> 00:17:59,967
how sign languages work first,
and we just haven't got the time.

236
00:17:59,968 --> 00:18:03,474
Yeah, you're right. It'd need a
whole presentation of its own.

237
00:18:03,498 --> 00:18:07,972
And what about screen time? Whether
toddlers pick up words from the television?

238
00:18:08,259 --> 00:18:11,960
I'd have said drop that, to be honest.
The research is pretty thin.

239
00:18:18,960 --> 00:18:22,948
But that's kind of the point, isn't it? Everyone assumes
educational programs help, and the evidence says they mostly don't.

240
00:18:22,972 --> 00:18:26,972
Not unless an adult is watching and talking about it with the
child. That's a nice surprising finding. Just a minute or two.

241
00:18:26,973 --> 00:18:30,370
All right, you win. And
then babbling, which

242
00:18:30,394 --> 00:18:33,972
obviously stays. It's
where everything starts.

243
00:18:34,060 --> 00:18:35,060
Definitely.

244
00:18:39,084 --> 00:18:43,120
Okay, then the main section. Child-directed
speech. You know, the way adults talk to babies,

245
00:18:43,144 --> 00:18:46,972
with the high pitch and the exaggerated tune.
Did anything in the reading surprise you?

246
00:18:52,960 --> 00:18:56,931
A couple of things. I'd always assumed it was mainly mothers
who did it, and that dads sort of talk to babies the same

247
00:18:56,955 --> 00:19:01,675
way they talk to anyone else. But that study showed fathers
change their voices just as much. That really surprised me.

248
00:19:02,960 --> 00:19:06,974
Hmm. What about the bit showing that
newborns would rather listen to it than

249
00:19:06,998 --> 00:19:10,972
to normal adult speech? I thought
that was amazing, at only a few days old.

250
00:19:20,960 --> 00:19:24,998
Honestly, that didn't really surprise me. Babies like anything that sounds a bit
musical. What did get me was that it isn't universal. I'd taken it for granted

251
00:19:25,022 --> 00:19:28,972
that every culture does it, but there are communities where adults hardly speak
directly to babies at all, and the kids still learn to talk perfectly well.

252
00:19:28,973 --> 00:19:32,514
Yes, that was striking. And
older brothers and sisters

253
00:19:32,538 --> 00:19:35,972
doing it to toddlers. I
found that quite unexpected.

254
00:19:36,209 --> 00:19:39,167
Not me. I've got a
four-year-old niece, and she

255
00:19:39,191 --> 00:19:41,972
talks to her baby
brother exactly like that.

256
00:19:41,973 --> 00:19:46,960
Fair enough. And the old claim
that it holds back vocabulary?

257
00:19:46,961 --> 00:19:50,982
Well, nobody really believes
that anymore. The studies show

258
00:19:51,006 --> 00:19:54,972
the opposite, which is pretty
much what I'd have expected.

259
00:19:55,559 --> 00:19:58,817
Before you hear the
rest of the discussion, you

260
00:19:58,841 --> 00:20:01,972
have some time to look
at questions 25 to 30.

261
00:20:32,409 --> 00:20:33,154
Now, about the video, I was
planning to show you a little bit

262
00:20:33,178 --> 00:20:34,885
of a little bit of a little bit
of a little bit of a little bit.

263
00:20:34,909 --> 00:20:37,810
The video. I was planning to show
that clip of my cousin's little boy.

264
00:20:37,834 --> 00:20:39,972
The one where he's naming
everything in the garden?

265
00:20:39,973 --> 00:20:43,960
It's lovely, but it's nearly
ten minutes long, isn't it?

266
00:20:44,109 --> 00:20:47,523
Oh, I'd only play a minute
of it. I can cut it down easily.

267
00:20:47,547 --> 00:20:50,972
And the sound's fine. I recorded
it on a decent microphone.

268
00:20:53,359 --> 00:20:57,121
But I've decided not to use it
after all. I mentioned it to my cousin,

269
00:20:57,145 --> 00:21:00,967
and she wasn't keen on her son
being shown to a room full of strangers.

270
00:21:00,968 --> 00:21:04,960
No, absolutely. That's her call.
We'll find something online instead.

271
00:21:09,259 --> 00:21:13,097
Then I want to do the WUG experiment. You know,
the one where kids are shown a drawing of a made-up

272
00:21:13,121 --> 00:21:16,972
creature called a WUG, and then two of them, and
they have to finish the sentence: "There are two…"

273
00:21:16,973 --> 00:21:20,960
Isn't it a bit old, though?
It was done in the 1950s.

274
00:21:26,109 --> 00:21:30,003
It's old, but it hasn't dated at all. I don't think
there's anything that shows more clearly that kids

275
00:21:30,027 --> 00:21:34,107
aren't just copying what they hear. They've never
heard the word WUGS, so they must be applying a rule.

276
00:21:34,209 --> 00:21:36,816
Some people say the results
were overstated, though.

277
00:21:36,840 --> 00:21:38,972
That a lot of the kids
were just guessing.

278
00:21:39,359 --> 00:21:41,596
I've read that critique,
and I don't buy it.

279
00:21:41,620 --> 00:21:43,972
The results have been
repeated loads of times.

280
00:21:43,973 --> 00:21:49,960
Okay. That leads nicely into
mistakes like "Eichut" or "two mouses".

281
00:21:50,109 --> 00:21:52,960
Yeah.
Parents get really worried about those.

282
00:21:52,961 --> 00:21:55,948
And they correct them
constantly, which, according

283
00:21:55,972 --> 00:21:58,967
to everything we read,
makes almost no difference.

284
00:21:58,968 --> 00:22:02,222
Right. And people think they've
stopped by about four, but

285
00:22:02,246 --> 00:22:05,972
actually some kids carry on
making them till they're six or seven.

286
00:22:09,960 --> 00:22:13,988
The point we need to get across is that they're
not a sign that anything's wrong. It's the reverse,

287
00:22:14,012 --> 00:22:17,972
really. The child has worked out the rule and is
just using it too widely. That's a step forward.

288
00:22:18,234 --> 00:22:21,405
Agreed. That's our key
message for that section.

289
00:22:21,429 --> 00:22:25,960
What about a handout? Last time
the tutor said ours was far too long.

290
00:22:25,961 --> 00:22:28,592
I'd rather not give
anything out at the start.

291
00:22:28,616 --> 00:22:30,972
People just read it
instead of listening.

292
00:22:31,509 --> 00:22:33,960
We could put all the
references on the final slide.

293
00:22:34,359 --> 00:22:37,960
Nobody can copy down 20
references in the 30 seconds it's up.

294
00:22:37,960 --> 00:22:42,960
Okay. So let's email a one-page summary
to everyone once the session's over.

295
00:22:43,359 --> 00:22:47,177
Yes. That's the best option. I'm
a bit worried about questions at

296
00:22:47,201 --> 00:22:50,972
the end, though. What if someone
asks something we can't answer?

297
00:22:57,259 --> 00:23:01,032
That doesn't bother me. We just say we'll look into it.
And we've left five minutes, which is plenty. What I'm more

298
00:23:01,056 --> 00:23:04,985
concerned about is what happened last term, when we asked
for questions and there was total silence. It was so awkward.

299
00:23:05,009 --> 00:23:10,960
Hannah. Oh, I remember.
So should we get a friend to ask something?

300
00:23:11,059 --> 00:23:13,968
Better than that. We'll
finish with a question for

301
00:23:13,992 --> 00:23:16,972
them to discuss in pairs.
That'll get them talking.

302
00:23:17,459 --> 00:23:20,166
Hannah. Right. So what
next? Should we book

303
00:23:20,190 --> 00:23:22,967
a meeting with Dr. Morgan
to check the plan?

304
00:23:22,968 --> 00:23:27,174
I don't think we need to. She said she's
happy as long as we stick to the outline.

305
00:23:27,198 --> 00:23:30,972
And we can't really run through it
properly till the slides are finished.

306
00:23:32,459 --> 00:23:36,495
Hannah. True. The problem is,
most of our sources are from the 90s.

307
00:23:36,519 --> 00:23:39,972
We need some recent research,
especially on bilingualism.

308
00:23:40,009 --> 00:23:43,609
Okay. Let's both spend tomorrow
in the library looking for studies

309
00:23:43,633 --> 00:23:46,972
from the last five years, and
then meet up again on Thursday.

310
00:23:47,709 --> 00:23:49,724
That is the end of part three.

